Scalar core stream transfers schedule like the cascade (Loom AIE2P descriptors): mov.ss / mov.ms were descriptor
barriers, so each one split its block into separately scheduled source ranges, and consecutive transfers waited the
previous one's full result latency (8 cycles after a read, 3 after a write) through the barrier effect's schedule-class
fallback. They now keep their order through named timing events (in order on one port, 1 cycle; independent of the
other port and of local memory, 0) and stay side-effecting, so they are never merged, hoisted or removed. A worker to
worker stream went from ~9.8 to ~3.6 cycles per word.
